A||N||2||normalicy||Yet Another Headlight Motor Question||||1||
Z||000000||normalicy||10-05-2001||07:54 PM||||I've read all the posts that I can find, and I can't quite figure weather my problem is the motor or something else.<P>What happens:  When I put the lights up, everything goes as planned.  However, when lowering, the motor on my drivers side spins continuously.  I'm not sure whether this is a relay or motor thing.  Whichever it may be, I need suggestions on the cheapest routes to fixing the problem.<P>Thanks,<BR>Mickey<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000001||72vinman||10-05-2001||08:37 PM||||I had the same problem. The gears inside the motor are stripped. I ordered the headlight rebuild kit from Rodney @ <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>  . It came with detailed instructions and took me about an hour and a half to do it. The only problem is the one I rebuilt is so much more smoother than the one that is original. Go see Rodney and you will be fine. In the meantime to stop it from spinning pull the plug next to the headlight assembly.||||reg||3
Z||000002||WampusCat||10-05-2001||08:39 PM||||Usually a motor that keeps spinning means that the drive gears are worn out, or the bushings if it's an 87-88.  The bushings are cheap and fairly easy to replace on the 87-88's.  I hear the 84-86's are more expensive and harder to fix.  Odd that it works in one direction and not the other though.  Rodney Dickman has the parts.||||reg||1
